    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d832">

                <pb facs="0954" n="842" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg842"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/372</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to President
                    Roosevelt</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-01-22">January 22, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. President</hi>: I am sending you here
                    attached: a copy of a despatch just received from Ambassador Grew<anchor n="40"
                        xml:id="d832-f1"/> on the subject: “The Importance of American Naval
                    Preparedness in connection with the Situation in the Far East”; a digest
                        thereof;<anchor n="41" xml:id="d832-f2"/> and a copy of a memorandum
                    prepared recently in the Department, entitled “Relations between the United
                    States and Countries of the Far East—Especially Japan—in 1935”.<anchor n="42"
                        xml:id="d832-f3"/></p>
                <pb facs="0955" n="843" xml:id="pg843"/>
                <p>The views expressed by Mr. Grew with regard to the present situation and the
                    importance of American naval preparedness are absolutely in accord with views
                    which have been expressed to me from time to time by my assistants who are
                    concerned with those questions here in the Department. With their deductions and
                    conclusions and those of Mr. Grew, I am absolutely in accord. In the memorandum
                    on “Relations … in 1935”, there are given suggestions with regard to the line of
                    procedure which this Government might to advantage follow, both in general and
                    with regard to particular questions, during the period immediately ahead of us.
                    Copies of this have been shown to Norman Davis<anchor n="43" xml:id="d832-f4"/>
                    and William Bullitt,<anchor n="44" xml:id="d832-f5"/> and they have both
                    expressed general concurrence.</p>
                <p>I think that you will wish to look through all of this, and at some time in the
                    near future I should like to discuss with you ways and means for bringing these
                    matters discreetly and in confidence to the attention of certain Members of the
                    Congress.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cordell Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
